---
import Footer from "@/components/site/footer.astro";
import Base from "@/layouts/base.astro";

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "what-is-kaji", text: "What is Kaji?" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "why-use-kaji", text: "Why use Kaji?" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "how-it-works", text: "How it works" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "ownership", text: "Ownership" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "what-kaji-is-not", text: "What Kaji is not" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Introduction"
  description="Kaji governs application actions requested by agents, automations, and other callers."
  activeHref="/introduction"
  headings={headings}
>
  <article class="article doc-article">
    <header>
      <h1>Introduction</h1>
      <p class="tagline">
        Kaji is an embedded execution layer for application actions that can create real side
        effects.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2 id="what-is-kaji">What is Kaji?</h2>
      <p>
        Your application already contains functions such as <code>refundPayment()</code>, <code
          >cancelBooking()</code
        >, and <code>sendEmail()</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kaji governs the point where an agent, automation, or direct caller requests one of these
        actions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kaji is a TypeScript library with zero runtime dependencies. It runs inside your
        application.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="why-use-kaji">Why use Kaji?</h2>
      <p>A state-changing action needs more than a function call.</p>
      <p>
        The application must validate input, identify the caller, enforce policy, coordinate
        duplicates, and preserve ambiguous outcomes.
      </p>
      <p>Kaji provides one execution path for those controls.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Define a capability with input validation, authorization, optional approval, and execution.</li>
        <li>Create a Kaji executor with an <code>ExecutionStore</code>.</li>
        <li>
          Call <code>kaji.execute()</code> with input, <code>principalId</code>, and an idempotency key.
        </li>
        <li>Kaji validates, claims, authorizes, obtains approval when required, and executes.</li>
        <li>
          Kaji returns one of six statuses: <code>succeeded</code>, <code>denied</code>, <code
            >rejected</code
          >, <code>failed</code>, <code>cancelled</code>, or <code>unknown</code>.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The configured store defines the coordination and durability scope. <code
          >memoryStore()</code
        > is process-local and non-durable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="ownership">Ownership</h2>
      <p>Kaji owns execution order, boundary enforcement, result classification, and minimal evidence.</p>
      <p>
        Your application owns identity, policy, approval systems, provider calls, production storage,
        reconciliation, and business audit data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="what-kaji-is-not">What Kaji is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not an agent framework.</strong> Kaji has no model provider or agent loop.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a workflow engine.</strong> Kaji does not orchestrate multi-step work.</li>
        <li><strong>Not an IAM system.</strong> The application supplies identity and policy.</li>
        <li><strong>Not an approval product.</strong> The application supplies the approval interface and decision logic.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a retry service.</strong> The application reconciles <code>unknown</code> outcomes.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a database.</strong> Production storage comes from your <code>ExecutionStore</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a distributed lock service or event store.</strong> The configured <code>ExecutionStore</code> owns claim coordination and outcome persistence.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a recovery worker.</strong> The application owns reconciliation after an <code>unknown</code> outcome.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="quickstart-links">
      <p>
        Continue with <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a> to define and run a
        capability.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</Base>
